description:
`adapter.run_throughput_test` (platforms/base/execution.py:1694-1714) always uses `_make_stream_cursor(connection)` and never calls `require_throughput_stream_capability`. PostgreSQL, Doris and SingleStore streams therefore share one psycopg/pymysql connection, and UNSUPPORTED platforms run anyway.

For TPC-DS it routes to the legacy `TPCDSBenchmark.run_throughput_test` (tpcds/benchmark/runner.py), which:
- orders queries with random.shuffle (:1527-1528), not dsqgen -STREAMS;
- uses one seed for every query in a stream (:1572);
- publishes Throughput@Size even when success=False (:1470-1490);
- shares one connection across streams (:1240-1242);
- computes power as 3600 x SF / T and publishes it even when queries fail (:1676-1693).

Deprecation messages at runner.py:56 and :1019 describe this API as spec-compliant.

The read-only decision item comment-cleanup-tpcds-stream-decision covers which ordering design to keep. This item covers the defects that exist regardless of that decision: the capability bypass, metrics published over failures, and the false compliance claims.
